  <section id="what-differs">
    <h2>How the systems differ</h2>
    <p>A <strong>standby generator</strong> is a permanent outdoor installation. When paired with compatible automatic controls and transfer equipment, it can detect a utility interruption, start, and switch the selected electrical loads. It still depends on a correctly sized fuel supply, commissioned system, and ongoing maintenance.</p>
    <p>A <strong>portable generator</strong> is brought out for use and must be operated outdoors. The owner must manage setup, fuel, operating conditions, and the connection to backed-up circuits. It does not become a safe or automatic standby system merely because it is large or uses a particular fuel.</p>
    <p class="mt-5">The diagram is a simplified explanation of the usual operating distinction, not wiring instructions. Exact system design belongs to the equipment manuals and a qualified installer.</p>
