  <section id="safety">
    <h2>Safety comes first</h2>
    <p>The CPSC’s May 2026 guidance says portable generators must be used outdoors at least 20 feet from homes and buildings, with exhaust directed away from windows, doors, and vents. Never run one inside a home, garage, basement, crawlspace, shed, or other enclosed space—even with openings left open. Install and maintain working carbon-monoxide alarms on every level and outside sleeping areas.</p>
    <p>For an electrical connection, never plug a generator into a household wall outlet. That can energize wiring in an unsafe way and create shock/fire risk. Ask a qualified electrician about transfer equipment appropriate to your panel and generator. Standby equipment also needs correct placement, exhaust management, and professional installation; it is not exempt from site-specific safety requirements.</p>
    <p>Read our <a href="/guides/generator-safety-carbon-monoxide/">carbon-monoxide safety guide</a> and <a href="/comparisons/transfer-switch-vs-interlock/">transfer-switch/interlock overview</a> before planning portable backup.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="cost-scope">
    <h2>Compare project scope, not just unit price</h2>
    <p>For a broad planning reference, HomeGuide lists a $6,000–$11,000 average installed cost for a whole-house generator and a $9,500–$24,000 range for longer distances or systems above 20 kW (published December 30, 2025). The same guide’s portable-generator equipment range is separate from costs for a transfer switch or connection work. Generac’s current guidance gives its own $8,000–$16,000 range including its generator, system equipment/materials, and installation. These sources use different scopes and market methods; neither is a quote for a particular home.</p>
    <p>See the <a href="/cost/standby-generator-cost/">standby-generator cost guide</a> for estimate scope and factors such as fuel-line capacity, conductor/trench distance, panel changes, permits, and site preparation.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="decision">
    <h2>Questions for your household</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Which loads must remain powered, and which can be turned off or sequenced?</li>
      <li>Does the property have a safe place that meets the equipment manual and local requirements?</li>
      <li>Can someone safely operate and refuel a portable unit during the outage conditions you expect?</li>
      <li>Would automatic transfer be necessary for medical equipment, water, heating, or long periods away from home?</li>
      <li>What does an itemized written quote include—and which items remain allowances or exclusions?</li>
    </ul>
    <p>There is no universal winner. Match the system to actual loads, outage conditions, safety capacity, and a complete installation plan—not to a generic ranking.</p>
  </section>
